---
// The standing health warning that sits at the top of every data view. The
// whole point of the section is that numbers travel with their caveats, so this
// is not decoration — it states the three things a reader must hold in mind
// before reading any figure below it.
---
<aside class="border border-ink-200 bg-paper-100 rounded-2 p-5 md:p-6 font-sans text-sm text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
  <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent mb-3">Before you read these numbers</p>
  <ul class="space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-900">It's not everywhere.</strong> The data covers the 43 forces of England &amp; Wales plus British Transport Police. Police Scotland and the PSNI don't publish here, so there is no UK total.</li>
    <li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-900">It's not now.</strong> Figures run two to three months behind, after court-result matching and anonymisation. The latest number is never the latest month.</li>
    <li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-900">It's not exact.</strong> Locations are snapped to anonymised map points to protect people, so the map is deliberately approximate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-ink-500">
    More on what these limits mean — and why they matter — in
    <a href="/other/reading-the-data" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-dark underline underline-offset-2">More or Less Policing</a>.
  </p>
</aside>
